[
  "[SCENE: Camera on desk setup. Screen capture ready.]\nHello, World.\nWelcome to Studio Workbench. I am Mike Hall.\nToday is not a polished presentation or a scripted lecture.\nI am sitting down at my desk to open up the workbench and show what I am preparing for, what has gone into this environment, and why I want to work in the open.\nI am not an auteur with all the answers.\nI am an engineer working through the process, learning about each topic as I go, through my own lived experiences and direct discovery.\n",
  "[SCENE: Switch to Workbench Terminal and OBS settings.]\nLet me show what has gone into getting this room and this machine ready.\nOn this Mac, everything runs local-first.\nWe have OBS Studio configured for four isolated 48 kilohertz audio tracks.\nMy Shure vocal microphone is on its own isolated channel.\nDesktop audio from Golden Gate and application audio from Chromium are strictly separated.\nVideo encoding uses Apple VideoToolbox hardware acceleration at full 1080p sixty frames per second.\nWhen a recording completes, it moves through local NVMe Maildir queues: staged, pending, processing, processed.\nThere is no manual file shuffling or disk bloat.\n",
  "[SCENE: Camera inset with terminal architecture diagram.]\nBehind this desk is an autonomous media pipeline running on a local Rails microservice.\nIt watches the ingress queues, extracts ephemeral audio, runs whisper.cpp for local transcription on Apple Silicon, and handles chunked resumable YouTube uploads with zero permanent disk waste.\nThe same engine powers three distinct efforts that serve different purposes.\nRamblin' and Amblin' captures freeform, walking thoughts straight from the phone.\nThe Sound Above is a structured oral history rewatch examining thirty-five interviews from the Chicago Software Craftsmanship era.\nAnd Studio Workbench is here at the desk: hands-on investigation, live debugging, and unvarnished problem solving.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the code editor and active project files.]\nSo why separate Studio Workbench from the rest?\nBecause the kinds of problems we hit at the desk need room to breathe.\nWhen I am tracking down an audio hum under high-tension power lines, or figuring out why a distributed transcription cluster stopped scaling, I do not want to force that into an oral history interview syllabus.\nThose are workbench investigations.\nThey belong here: looking at waveforms, testing hypotheses, reading logs, and seeing what breaks.\nSometimes I have a clear destination in mind.\nSometimes I am figuring out the point as I go.\n",
  "[SCENE: Camera on host.]\nThat is the heart of process cinema.\nIn software craftsmanship, we talk a lot about deliberate practice.\nPractice is not performing what you already know perfectly for an audience.\nPractice is being willing to expose your ignorance, test a small experiment, see it fail, and adjust.\nIn an industry that currently hypes synthetic perfection, I find real comfort in working through genuine problems where you can see the seams.\nIf something fails on this desk, you will see it fail.\nAnd if we find a clean solution, you will see exactly why it worked.\n",
  "[SCENE: Outro lower third and closing resources.]\nThat is the setup.\nThe pipeline is connected. The audio is locked. The workbench is open.\nIn the sessions ahead, we will take on audio DSP forensics, distributed systems, system cartography, and live agent pairing.\nAll code, notes, and transcripts will be linked on just3ws.com.\nThank you for spending time with me at the bench today.\nLet us get to work.\n"
]